    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "In this work, modeling and upscaling approaches for nonconventional wells (NCWs), i.e., wells with general trajectories and an arbitrary number of branches, are developed and applied. The modeling of such wells is complicated by their geometry as well as by their interaction with local reservoir heterogeneity.A semianalytical well model based on Green's functions is extended by a novel approximation for near wellbore heterogeneity. The so-called s-k* heterogeneity model represents permeability variation via a locally varying skin s and an effective background permeability k*. The accuracy of this method is demonstrated relative to detailed finite difference simulations for several well geometries and types of permeability fields. The semianalytical tool is efficient and is applied to give productivity estimates for multiple permeability realizations in the presence of wellbore pressure effects. The s-k* model is also used to simplify and coarsen two-phase finite difference simulations.Well indices for use in a finite difference simulator are then computed using the semianalytical technique. The implementation is new and quite general in that the interaction of multiple wells in a user-defined local well domain is considered for any type of simulation grid. The method is applied to globally unstructured and Cartesian grids and compared to conventional approaches. It is further demonstrated that, for heterogeneous cases, the method approximates subgrid permeability information for coarse grids.Finite difference models can treat reservoir effects (e.g., multiphase flow, heterogeneity) more rigorously than semianalytical models. A local well model for use with a finite difference method based on globally unstructured multiblock grids is introduced. Here the well trajectory is accurately resolved by the simulation grid. Near-well upscaling is introduced by adjusting the well model such that a reference fine grid heterogeneous solution is matched. The method is shown to provide accurate coarse grid results for single and two-phase flow problems. Taken in total, the methods presented in this thesis provide a number of new and useful approaches for the accurate modeling of complex nonconventional wells.",
